  "raw": "\n000\nFXUS61 KBTV 230627\nAFDBTV\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Burlington VT\n227 AM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nAs of 227 AM EDT Friday...\n\nNo significant changes have been made with this forecast package.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\nAs of 227 AM EDT Friday...\n\n1. Dry and seasonable weather to continue through early next \nweek.\n\n2. Dry cold front to bring cooler temperatures on Tuesday and \nWednesday with dry weather now expected to continue through much of \nnext week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nAs of 227 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure will continue to build across the North \nCountry and northern New York today with the center of the high \ndirectly overhead on Saturday and the first half of Sunday. Below \nnormal high temperatures, in the 48-52 degree range, are expected \ntoday with plentiful sunshine. Temperatures will moderate through \nthe weekend with high temperatures by Sunday warming into the lower \n60s. Dry weather will continue through Monday with the lack of any \nappreciable moisture or significant changes in the synoptic longwave \npattern.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Models continue to be less bullish on the upcoming cold \nfront later on Monday. The latest suite of global guidance is \nshowing a plethora of dry air ahead of the front which should allow \nthe front to pass through the region with virtually no \nprecipitation. A few members of the GEFS and ECMWF ensemble are \nshowing a hundredth or two of precip at a few locations but the\ntrend continues to be drier which makes sense given the current\npattern. Another sharp cool down will be seen as we head into \nFriday with high temperatures struggling to warm into the mid to\nupper 40s even with abundant sunshine. Another 1030+ mb high \npressure system will settle into the region on Tuesday with dry \nweather now expected through much of the week. A slight chance \nof rain has been added for Friday with a more amplified pattern \nwith some moisture advection ahead of a stronger cold front. The\ndry air mass in place will be hard to displace so we will see \nhow this pans out in a week from now.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nThrough 06Z Saturday...VFR conditions will prevail through the TAF\nperiod. Winds will remain out of the north through the forecast\nperiod at 5 to 7 knots during the day and much lighter during the\novernight period. Some gusty winds to 15 knots will be seen in the\nChamplain Valley for a few hours this afternoon.\n\nOutlook...\n\nSaturday: VFR. NO SIG WX.\nSaturday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.\nSunday: VFR. NO SIG WX.\nSunday Night: VFR. NO SIG WX.\nMonday: Mainly VFR, with local MVFR possible. NO SIG WX.\nMonday Night: Mainly VFR, with local MVFR possible. NO SIG WX.\nTuesday: VFR. NO SIG WX.\n\n&&\n\n.BTV WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nVT...None.\nNY...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nWHAT HAS CHANGED...Clay\nDISCUSSION...Clay\nAVIATION...Clay\n"
